  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Spirostan-3,27-diol, (3alpha)- and U373 MG, mediated through the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is identified as a target of multiple compounds, including (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one, 17,3-(Epoxymethano)-1,17:2,6-dimethano-17H-naphtho(1,2-f)furo(3,4-b:2,3-c')bisoxocin-4,8,11,21(1H,8aH,10bH)-tetrone, 2,3,6,6a,9,10,10a,15,16,16a-decahydro-8a,15-dihydroxy-2,6a,10b-trimethyl-, (1S,2S,3S,6R,6aS,8aS,10aS,10bS,16aR,17R,18aR)-, and Palmyramide A. These compounds all share a common target, the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which in turn is linked to U373 MG. The scores from the three paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with varying degrees of support from different compounds. The evidence suggests that Spirostan-3,27-diol, (3alpha)- may act as a modulator or activator of the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which then influences the growth of U373 MG. The mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple targets in the pathway, indicating a potential role in cellular processes that could be relevant to the biology of U373 MG. The relationship is supported by the cumulative evidence from the three paths, but further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct interaction between Spirostan-3,27-diol, (3alpha)- and U373 MG.",
